{
  "dom_id": "step-q345223",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "A body of mass $\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}).$  At time $t$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=\\operatorname{sec}\u2061t$ meters per second. \n\nCalculate the magnitude of the force at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ seconds. Do not round the answer.\n\nThe magnitude of the force equals\n{{field-1}} $\\text{N}.$",
  "worked_solution": "According to Newton\u2019s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sec}\u2061t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}\u2061t\\operatorname{tan}\u2061t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the acceleration at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,\\text{s}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{sec}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{tan}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =ma(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the magnitude of the force is\n\n$$\n|F(\\frac{\\pi }{4})|=|2|=2\\,\\text{N}.\n$$",
